# {{KTM_CREATE_PROJECT_NAME}} C++ SDK guide

This project was generated from the Kinematic Trees kt-node C++ SDK boilerplate. It is a normal editable CMake project plus KTM metadata for native dependency resolution and packaging.

## Project layout

- `include/{{KTM_CREATE_MODULE_NAME}}/robot.hpp` — public project API.
- `src/robot.cpp` — implementation that calls kt-node native ABI functions.
- `examples/basic.cpp` — minimal executable example.
- `tests/smoke.cpp` — smoke test for headers, library link, and runtime loading.
- `CMakeLists.txt` — CMake build definition for library, example, and smoke test.
- `scripts/build.sh` — builds the shared library and writes payload to `$KTM_BUILD_OUTPUT/compiled` plus source snapshot to `$KTM_BUILD_OUTPUT/source`.
- `ktm-pack.json` — editable package-owned pack mode definition.

## Using the SDK

Run through KTM so CMake sees kt-node include/library paths and runtime loader paths:

```bash
ktm doctor --platform {{KTM_CREATE_PRIMARY_PLATFORM}}
ktm dev --platform {{KTM_CREATE_PRIMARY_PLATFORM}}
ktm test --platform {{KTM_CREATE_PRIMARY_PLATFORM}}
```

KTM also supplies the package-owned `KTMessages::KTMessages` CMake target.
Include generated datatypes from `kt/messages`; do not copy generated headers
into the project or SDK.

In project code:

```cpp
#include "{{KTM_CREATE_MODULE_NAME}}/robot.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  {{KTM_CREATE_MODULE_NAME}}::Robot robot;
  std::cout << robot.status() << "\n";
}
```

`Robot::status()` reports the loaded kt-node ABI version.

## Manual C++ workflow

Prefer KTM commands. If you need to run CMake directly, export the KTM environment first:

```bash
eval "$(ktm env --shell)"
cmake -S . -B build/manual -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build/manual
ctest --test-dir build/manual --output-on-failure
```

## Packing modes

- `compiled` packages only `compiled/`, including library output and public headers.
- `compiled-source` composes `compiled` plus `source/`, a separate canonical source snapshot. If composed lists overlap, KTM deduplicates paths deterministically before packaging.

Docs are copied into generated projects for humans/agents, but are intentionally not packaged by default.
## Dependency ownership / eject review

The default pack modes keep `kinematic-trees/kt-node@{{KTM_CREATE_KT_NODE_VERSION}}` as a KTM-managed dependency. A normal compiled or compiled-source package must not copy KTM store paths, dependency shared libraries, headers, or metadata into the project payload.

A future eject/self-contained flow should be explicit and separately reviewed because it changes who owns dependency updates, license notices, SBOM evidence, and security patching. Until that flow exists, `ktm pack --mode eject` is expected to fail rather than silently vendor dependencies.

## Quick payload inspection

After packing, inspect contents with:

```bash
tar -tzf _pack/*/{{KTM_CREATE_PRIMARY_PLATFORM}}/*.tar.gz | sort
```

Expected defaults:

- `compiled`: only runtime/compiled payload roots.
- `compiled-source`: runtime/compiled payload roots plus separate source-list roots.
- `docs/`: present in the generated project folder, absent from archives by default.
- `deps/`, `.ktrees/`, KTM store paths, and kt-node dependency libraries: absent from normal pack archives.
